u}(	ext{radiative}))$ exhibit significant departures from their asymptotic heavy quark limit predictions. These anomalies arise from substantial contributions by the subleading Isgur-Wise function, which are further amplified by significant corrections from non-perturbative effects in QCD. Consequently, these findings suggest that the long-standing experimental efforts to test Isgur-Wise universality may yield inconclusive results.

The LHCb collaboration has recently reported significant deviations from the Standard Model in the $F^0 	o D^+ e^-  ar{
u}_e$ and $D^0 	o D^+ e^-  ar{
u}_e$ angular distributions. Should these anomalies gain confirmation, they would represent the first compelling evidence of physics beyond the Standard Model. This study explores the implications of such a discovery for semileptonic $B^0 	o D^{*+} \mu^-  ar{
u}_\mu$ decays, specifically investigating how a new physics contribution to the vector form factor could be extracted. We demonstrate that, under the hypothesis of a new physics effect in the vector form factor, the LHCb experiment could determine the new physics scale with a precision of approximately 10%.
